      <Option type="QString" value="{19665173-e64a-4863-a153-1c7db4fe8e04}" name="uuid"/>
    </Option>
    <Option type="Map">
      <Option type="QString" value="226,178,74,255" name="color"/>
      <Option type="QString" value="Posthole &quot;ShpType&quot; = '06' / '61'&#xa;_________________________&#xa;The '06' /'61' shptype represents postholes, allowing the measurement of multiple successive, size-similar postholes (e.g., fence posts) through a single measurement taken at the feature’s centroid.&#xa;The posthole diameter is indicated by the letter immediately preceding '61' in the 10-character ArchSurv code. The diameter values correspond to letters 'A' through 'Z' as follows:&#xa;&#xa;A = 1 cm&#xa;B = 2 cm&#xa;C = 3 cm&#xa;...&#xa;Z = 26 cm diameter&#xa;&#xa;Example:&#xa;0037E61003 — represents the third posthole in layer 37 with a diameter of 5 cm (letter E = 5 cm)." name="component_description"/>
      <Option type="double" value="351.1358567178817" name="component_height"/>
      <Option type="double" value="1502.5374948904719" name="component_pos_x"/>
      <Option type="double" value="823.4485229397864" name="component_pos_y"/>
      <Option type="double" value="631.7175175788258" name="component_width"/>
      <Option type="bool" value="true" name="outputs_collapsed"/>
      <Option type="bool" value="true" name="parameters_collapsed"/>
      <Option type="QString" value="{1f997ad6-af76-4d9e-8f0b-0467d449724f}" name="uuid"/>
    </Option>
    <Option type="Map">
      <Option type="QString" value="" name="color"/>
      <Option type="QString" value="Polygon Generation #1" name="component_description"/>
      <Option type="double" value="312.08933137265325" name="component_height"/>
      <Option type="double" value="2238.818227301877" name="component_pos_x"/>
      <Option type="double" value="2522.5431732131265" name="component_pos_y"/>
      <Option type="double" value="324.7550972166269" name="component_width"/>
      <Option type="bool" value="true" name="outputs_collapsed"/>
      <Option type="bool" value="true" name="parameters_collapsed"/>
      <Option type="QString" value="{28c17d5d-5f7c-429d-9875-1e22cacb0611}" name="uuid"/>
    </Option>
    <Option type="Map">
      <Option type="QString" value="253,191,111,255" name="color"/>
      <Option type="QString" value="What is ArchSurv?&#xa;_____________________&#xa;ArchSurv is a 10-character text encoding system designed for archaeologists to record point measurements directly in-field,  which can then be automatically mapped into excavation plans during post-processing in-office.&#xa;&#xa;&quot;ArchSurv-String&quot; consists of four components 'XXXX'/'Y'/'ZZ'/'001' [e.g. '0457B03046']:&#xa;&#xa;'XXXX' - 4 digits: feature number ('0457') --> &quot;ID&quot;&#xa;'Y' - 1 letter: line container / point property ('B') &#xa;'ZZ' - 2 digits: shape type ('03')&#xa;'001' - 3 digits: sequence number ('046')&#xa;&#xa;ArchSurv-String supports the creation of various geometry types used for spatial recording of archaeological features,  such as points,  polylines,  and polygons.&#xa;&#xa;feature | '01' - heights, '02' - polyline, '03' - polygon&#xa;sample | '51' - sample point, '52' - sample polyline, '53' - sample polygon&#xa;posthole | '61' - buffered point measurement&#xa;finds | '71' - find point, '72' - find polyline, '73' - find polygon&#xa;3D markers | '81' - 3D marker point&#xa;trench | '91' - fixed point, '92' - trench polyline, '93' trench polygon&#xa;section nails | '00' - section nail point&#xa;&#xa;&quot;Code&quot;-Field: Many surveying instruments allow users to specify both a &quot;Point ID&quot; and an additional point attribute during measurement. In AS4QGIS, this attribute is called the &quot;Code&quot;. In archaeological contexts, it can be used, for example, to indicate the excavation trench in which a feature is located or to classify the type of feature." name="component_description"/>
      <Option type="double" value="567.1368336579301" name="component_height"/>
      <Option type="double" value="2007.9113153724304" name="component_pos_x"/>
      <Option type="double" value="347.55973044428754" name="component_pos_y"/>
      <Option type="double" value="646.571077679763" name="component_width"/>
      <Option type="bool" value="true" name="outputs_collapsed"/>
      <Option type="bool" value="true" name="parameters_collapsed"/>
      <Option type="QString" value="{3c2c0c79-9ceb-444c-9d73-92e51bca0f44}" name="uuid"/>
    </Option>
    <Option type="Map">
      <Option type="QString" value="242,242,242,255" name="color"/>
      <Option type="QString" value="end of initial string parsing and geometry filtering / start of the four geometry pipelines&#xa;" name="component_description"/>
      <Option type="double" value="133.48352243862138" name="component_height"/>
      <Option type="double" value="1492.0761278244033" name="component_pos_x"/>
      <Option type="double" value="2274.869405183828" name="component_pos_y"/>
      <Option type="double" value="2873.662247407139" name="component_width"/>
      <Option type="bool" value="true" name="outputs_collapsed"/>
      <Option type="bool" value="true" name="parameters_collapsed"/>
      <Option type="QString" value="{4c5c6929-3caf-4baa-a998-821af476de24}" name="uuid"/>
    </Option>
    <Option type="Map">
      <Option type="QString" value="252,126,126,255" name="color"/>
      <Option type="QString" value="CAD Export (DXF)&#xa;Currently, this pipeline does not include native .dxf export because the QGIS Model Designer’s .dxf export algorithm does not support CAD layer structuring based on the attribute field &quot;CAD-ID&quot;. Instead, it exports all data into a single CAD layer named after the shapefile.&#xa;&#xa;HOWEVER&#xa;You can perform .dxf export using the native QGIS built-in algorithm available under:&#xa;Project → Import/Export → DXF Export&#xa;&#xa;Select the layers you want to export.&#xa;On the right, each shapefile has a dropdown menu for choosing the attribute used for CAD layer grouping.&#xa;&#xa;Recommended settings:&#xa;Polylines: Choose either &quot;ID&quot; (e.g., '1', '2', '99', '270', '9567') or &quot;CAD-ID&quot; (e.g., '0001PS', '0002VF', '0099BS', '0270OB', '9567OF').&#xa;Polygons: Link the attribute field &quot;ID&quot; to your database, filter polygons by dating or other criteria, then export.&#xa;Pointdata: Export either by &quot;CAD-ID&quot; to merge point data and polylines into one layer, or by &quot;shptype&quot; to cluster points by categories such as finds, fixed points, photogrammetry markers, etc.&#xa;Vertices: Being the largest dataset, it is recommended to either skip exporting vertices or export them under their shapefile name.&#xa;_____________________&#xa;Recommendation:&#xa;For daily data saving/output, export only polylines. Export point data, vertices, and polygons to the CAD project file only prior to publication.&#xa;_____________________&#xa;Known Issues and Solutions&#xa;After export, all text entities have a Z-value of 0.&#xa;Workaround: Select all text and use the snapping tool to move all text entities simultaneously in 3D to the correct elevation.&#xa;" name="component_description"/>
      <Option type="double" value="583.6177568236492" name="component_height"/>
      <Option type="double" value="2383.442136915187" name="component_pos_x"/>
      <Option type="double" value="1894.8513234120446" name="component_pos_y"/>
